  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>Every registered care service in Scotland completes a <strong>Care Inspectorate annual return</strong>. It is a standard part of being registered, and it gives the Care Inspectorate an up-to-date picture of each service so it can plan its scrutiny and understand the sector. For most managers the return is not hard in principle. It is hard because the information is scattered, and pulling it together at the deadline turns a summary into a scramble.</p><p>The exact fields the return asks for are set by the Care Inspectorate and change from time to time. The return broadly asks about your service, your staffing and your quality; always check the Care Inspectorate's current requirements for what is asked and when it is due. Treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>Every registered care service in Scotland completes an annual return for the Care Inspectorate; it is part of being registered.</li><li>The return broadly asks about your service, your staffing and your quality, and it feeds the Care Inspectorate's picture of the sector and its scrutiny planning.</li><li>The exact fields and deadline are set by the Care Inspectorate and change over time, so always check the current requirements.</li><li>The return is easy when self-evaluation and routine checks have been captured through the year, and painful when they have to be reconstructed from memory.</li><li>Keeping the evidence current turns the return into a summary of records you already hold.</li><li>Quickler captures self-evaluation and checks at the point of work. It is not a care record, an eMAR or a statutory-submission system, and it does not file the return for you.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"What it is","heading":"What the annual return is for","html":"<p>The annual return is how the Care Inspectorate keeps an up-to-date record of every registered service in Scotland. It broadly gathers information about the service itself, about the staff team, and about the quality of care, so the regulator can understand the sector, plan its inspections and spot where support or scrutiny is needed. It is not the inspection, and it is not the self-evaluation, but it draws on the same underlying information about how the service runs and how well it is doing.</p><p>Because the specific fields, format and timing are set by the Care Inspectorate and updated from time to time, this page does not list them. Always work from the Care Inspectorate's current requirements for the return, including what information is asked for and by when it must be submitted. Treat this page as general guidance on staying ready, not as a definition of the return.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Why it hurts","heading":"Why the return is painful, and why it need not be","html":"<p>The return itself is a set of questions. What makes it painful is answering them from a standing start. If the year's checks live in a mix of paper folders, spreadsheets and people's memories, the run-up to the deadline becomes a reconstruction exercise: chasing dates, hunting for the photo, working out whether a policy was actually reviewed. The information exists, but not in a form you can lift straight into the return.</p><p>The fix is not to work harder at the deadline. It is to capture the year as it happens. A self-evaluation kept current against the quality framework, and routine checks recorded at the point of work with dates and photos, mean the annual return is answered from records you already hold. The deadline stops being a scramble and becomes an afternoon of pulling a summary together.</p>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Scope, honestly","heading":"What Quickler does and does not do","html":"<p>Quickler captures the self-evaluation walk and routine checks over WhatsApp, at the point you observe them, and keeps them in a searchable record with dates and photos. When the annual return is due, the information it asks about is already gathered rather than reconstructed. For an organisation running several services, a manager sees each one's position on a live dashboard.</p><p>What Quickler does not do: it does not file the annual return for you, and it is not the Care Inspectorate's submission system. It is not a care management system, an eMAR or a clinical record, so it does not hold personal plans, medication records or personal care logs. Those stay in your care planning system as the single source of truth. Quickler is the evidence and evaluation layer around them, and you complete the return through the Care Inspectorate's own process. Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Care Inspectorate, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>What is the Care Inspectorate annual return?</h3><p>It is a return every registered care service in Scotland completes for the Care Inspectorate. It broadly asks about your service, your staffing and your quality, and it gives the regulator an up-to-date picture of the sector to inform its scrutiny. The exact fields and deadline are set by the Care Inspectorate and change from time to time, so always check the current requirements. Treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p><h3>When is the annual return due?</h3><p>The timing is set by the Care Inspectorate and can change, so check its current requirements for the window and deadline that apply to your service. The practical point is that the return is far easier when your self-evaluation and routine checks have been captured through the year, whatever the deadline turns out to be.</p><h3>How do I keep the information ready for the return?</h3><p>Capture it as the year happens rather than reconstructing it at the deadline. Keep an honest self-evaluation current against the quality framework, and record routine environment, staffing and quality checks at the point of work with dates and photos. Then the return is answered from records you already hold. Quickler is one way to capture that evidence over WhatsApp.</p><h3>Does Quickler submit the annual return for me?</h3><p>No. Quickler captures and keeps the evidence the return asks about, but it does not file the return and it is not the Care Inspectorate's submission system. You complete the return through the Care Inspectorate's own process. Quickler is £20 per active user per month, with a £20 minimum. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so dormant users are free and you only pay for who actually works. There is a free tier of 20 reports per month with up to 10 photos per report. Quickler is not a care record or care management system.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-self-evaluation-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate self-evaluation guide</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-self-evaluation-template-scotland.html\">Self-evaluation template</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-complaints-notifications-scotland.html\">Complaints and notifications</a></li></ul>"}
